e,v=ev(62,'45–49','Onlookers conjecture that former merit caused the destination. Yogins leave meditation and ask the Gaṇas how these grave wrongdoers gained a rare blessed end despite no visible piety. They would abandon their own observance to do that method: after uncounted dispassionate, air-fed austerity they still have not seen the deity and ask when his abode will be theirs.',[('Temple yogins in62','FAMILY'),('Gaṇeśa-shaped rescuing Gaṇas in62','FAMILY'),('Festival townspeople in62','FAMILY')],story='gup5-story-durva-frame',outcome='An explicit efficacy question opens the Gaṇas’ nested dūrvā explanation; the outer conversation continues.')
K='Kauṇḍinya (dūrvā teacher)';AŚ='Aśrayā (Kauṇḍinya wife)'
e,v=ev(63,'1–7','The rescuing Gaṇas tell the yogins that even Śeṣa and four-faced Brahmā cannot fully describe Gaṇeśa, whom Brahmā and Śiva praise. Dūrvā’s greatness is not known even to sages/gods and cannot be attained by sacrifices, gifts, tapas, vows or homa. They introduce an earlier Indra–Nārada conversation: Indra honours the visiting sage, seats him and asks why dūrvā is especially dear to Gaṇeśa.',[('Gaṇeśa-shaped rescuing Gaṇas in62','FAMILY'),('Temple yogins in62','FAMILY'),('Śeṣa',D),('Brahmā',D),('Śiva',D),('Indra',D),('Nārada',S),('Dūrvā',C)],story='gup5-story-durva-frame',outcome='The Gaṇas’ explanation embeds Indra–Nārada, which next embeds Kauṇḍinya’s teaching.')
e,v=ev(63,'8–15','Nārada describes the sage Kauṇḍinya in Sthāvara city, with a wooded hermitage south of the settlement and lotus lakes populated by birds and other creatures. He meditates and performs intense tapas before a happy, boon-giving four-armed Gaṇeśa image covered in dūrvā, japping the six-letter mantra. Wife Aśrayā asks why he offers loads of mere grass daily when nobody is ordinarily pleased with grass, and what merit it gives.',[('Nārada',S),(K,S),(AŚ,F),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Dūrvā',C),('Six-syllable Gaṇeśa mantra',T),('Sthāvara city in63',L),('Kauṇḍinya dūrvā hermitage',L)],story='gup5-story-durva-frame',outcome='Explicit sage, wife, ritual image/mantra and efficacy question introduced; this sage is distinct from the earlier city named Kauṇḍinya.')
rel(AŚ,'spouse_of',K,e,'MARITAL');rel(K,'worships','Gaṇeśa',e);rel(K,'instructs',AŚ,e)
e,v=ev(63,'16–22','Kauṇḍinya recounts a festival in Dharma’s city with gods, Gandharvas, Apsarases, Siddhas, Cāraṇas, Nāgas, sages, Yakṣas and Rākṣasas. Dancing Tilottamā’s covering falls; Yama sees her breasts and desires to embrace and kiss her. Ashamed, he leaves the assembly; his semen falls on the ground. From it arises a flame-garlanded, distorted-faced, cruel man with terrible sounds, burning earth and reaching the sky with his hair, terrifying the three worlds.',[(K,S),(AŚ,F),('Yama',D),('Tilottamā',F),('Analāsura',A),('Dharma festival city in63',L),('Gods',C),('Gandharvas',C),('Apsarases',C),('Siddhas',C),('Cāraṇas',C),('Nāgas',C),('Sages',C),('Yakṣas',C),('Rākṣasas',C)],story='gup5-story-analasura',outcome='Explicit semen-origin of the later named fire-demon; no intercourse with Tilottamā is narrated.')
rel('Analāsura','semen_origin_from','Yama',e,'SOURCE_NARRATED_SEMEN_ORIGIN')
power('Analāsura',e,v,mechanism='Flame-covered form burns earth and terrorizes three worlds with appearance and sounds.',notes='Actual destructive capacity; no boon source is supplied here.')
e,v=ev(63,'23–31','The assembly goes to Viṣṇu, hymns him and seeks help for the world. Viṣṇu goes with them to Gaṇeśa, recognized as capable of destroying the threat. Gods and sages praise obstacle-making/removing, universal witness, world-origin/protection/destruction and wish-granting qualities, asking what other refuge could remove fear. They lament an untimely dissolution and ask why the deity neglects them when all face death.',[('Viṣṇu',D),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Gods',C),('Sages',C),('Analāsura',A)],story='gup5-story-analasura',outcome='The threat produces a collective refuge plea through Viṣṇu.')
e,v=ev(63,'32–42','Gaṇeśa compassionately appears as a beautiful, strongly radiant child with two arms reaching the knees, fine limbs, ornaments and costly clothes. The gods/sages rise, acclaim and bow, ask his identity and purpose, and infer Brahman’s child-form has come to rescue those who abandoned rites through Analāsura fear. The smiling child confirms their understanding and says he voluntarily came to kill the harmful demon.',[('Gaṇeśa',D),('Gaṇeśa child form against Analāsura','MANIFESTATION'),('Gods',C),('Sages',C),('Analāsura',A)],story='gup5-story-analasura',outcome='Two-armed child manifestation explicitly announces the rescue purpose.')
rel('Gaṇeśa child form against Analāsura','manifestation_of','Gaṇeśa',e);val('Gaṇeśa child form against Analāsura','manifest_form','Beautiful two-armed child, arms reaching knees, radiant and adorned63.32–36.',e)
e,v=ev(63,'43–51','The child announces a means of killing the demon and directs the gods to carry it out. The following strategy clause is preserved as sarvair bhavadbhis taṃ dṛṣṭvā nodanīyo balād aham; its exact intended action remains unresolved. He tells them to watch his and the demon’s remarkable encounter. They joyfully yet uncertainly discuss his capacity and bow. Analāsura approaches as dissolution-fire, burning ten directions and devouring humans. The people cry out and sages flee, warning the child to escape before the fire kills him, comparing it with a great predator. Gaṇeśa stands firm like Himavat; they retreat far away, leaving him there.',[('Gaṇeśa',D),('Gaṇeśa child form against Analāsura','MANIFESTATION'),('Analāsura',A),('Gods',C),('Sages',C),('Humans threatened byAnalāsura','FAMILY')],story='gup5-story-analasura',outcome='The appointed encounter begins; the child remains after the rescuers flee. The precise preceding strategy is withheld.')
e,v=ev(64,'1–8','Aśrayā asks Kauṇḍinya what occurred between the steadfast child and the fire-demon. Through Nārada’s narration to Indra, Kauṇḍinya says the advancing demon shakes earth and mountains, roars like thunder, drops birds from branches, uproots trees and leaves the sea without water. The child seizes the fiery demon by māyā power and swallows him before everyone, like the jar-born sage swallowing the ocean.',[(AŚ,F),(K,S),('Nārada',S),('Indra',D),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Gaṇeśa child form against Analāsura','MANIFESTATION'),('Analāsura',A),('Jar-born sage in64 comparison','EPITHET')],story='gup5-story-analasura',outcome='The demon is actually captured and swallowed; subsequent danger is internal fire.')
power('Gaṇeśa',e,v,mechanism='Māyā-powered seizure and swallowing of Analāsura.',trigger='The demon’s approach to the steadfast child.')
e,v=ev(64,'9–16','Gaṇeśa considers that the demon entering his belly could burn the three worlds seen inside. Indra gives Moon to calm the fire; gods and sages praise Bhālacandra, but the throat-fire persists. Brahmā gives his mentally created maidens Siddhi and Buddhi, described through cooling lotus/water/Moon imagery, and tells him to embrace them. The embrace calms the fire only slightly. Viṣṇu gives a tender lotus, bringing Padmapāṇi praise; Varuṇa pours cold water; Śiva gives a thousand-hooded serpent binding the belly, giving the name Vyālabaddhodara. The throat is still not cooled.',[('Gaṇeśa',D),('Indra',D),('Moon',D),('Brahmā',D),('Siddhi (dakṣiṇā maiden)',F),('Buddhi (dakṣiṇā maiden)',F),('Viṣṇu',D),('Varuṇa',D),('Śiva',D),('Cooling lotus fromViṣṇu in64',C),('Thousand-hood serpent fromŚiva in64','CONCEPT'),('Bhālacandra cooling-context name','EPITHET'),('Padmapāṇi cooling-context name','EPITHET'),('Vyālabaddhodara name in64','EPITHET')],story='gup5-story-analasura',outcome='Named cooling attempts have specific donors and limited or failed outcomes, not a single successful counter.')
rel('Siddhi (dakṣiṇā maiden)','mentally_created_by','Brahmā',e,'MENTAL_CREATION');rel('Buddhi (dakṣiṇā maiden)','mentally_created_by','Brahmā',e,'MENTAL_CREATION')
for n in ['Bhālacandra cooling-context name','Padmapāṇi cooling-context name','Vyālabaddhodara name in64']:rel(n,'source_epithet_of','Gaṇeśa',e)
power('Gaṇeśa',e,v,mechanism='Indra gives Moon, Brahmā gives mentally created Siddhi/Buddhi for an embrace, Viṣṇu gives lotus, Varuṇa cold water and Śiva a thousand-hood serpent. Moon/water/serpent do not settle the throat-fire; the embrace calms only slightly.',counter='The internal fire remains after these attempts.',notes='These are narrative gifts/counter-attempts, not a requested personal boon exchange. No marriage ceremony is supplied in this locus.')
e,v=ev(64,'17–23','Eighty-eight thousand sages arrive, each placing twenty-one nectar-like dūrvā sprouts on the deity’s head. The fire finally subsides and Gaṇeśa is pleased. Knowing this, gods and sages offer more dūrvā. He declares even great or small devoted worship fruitless without dūrvā, and none gains its worship-fruit without it. Devotional offering of one or twenty-one at dawn gives a fruit not attained even by hundreds of sacrifices, gifts, vow accumulations, severe tapas or rules earned over crores of births.',[('Gaṇeśa',D),('Eighty-eight thousand cooling sages','FAMILY'),('Gods',C),('Sages',C),('Dūrvā',C),('Dūrvā-offering practitioners','FAMILY')],story='gup5-story-analasura',outcome='Exact eighty-eight-thousand/each-twenty-one cooling succeeds and prompts the dūrvā requirement.')
power('Eighty-eight thousand cooling sages',e,v,mechanism='Each offers21dūrvā on the head and the internal fire subsides.',trigger='The88,000sages’ combined offerings.',notes='The count is per sage, not21total or a silently multiplied new total.')
power('Dūrvā-offering practitioners',e,v,mechanism='Great worship-fruit exceeding the enumerated other accumulated practices.',trigger='Devotional dawn offering of one or21dūrvā.',restrictions='Great or small devoted worship without dūrvā is said to be fruitless.')
e,v=ev(64,'24–31','The gods worship again with dūrvā. The joyful deity roars, grants many unspecified boons and withdraws his child form. They call him Kālānalapraśamana, install an image in a built temple and name it Vighnahara. Victory gives the city the name Vijaya; obstacles being removed explains Vighnahartṛ. Bathing, giving, tapas and observance there become inexhaustible through his favour. Kauṇḍinya closes the account to Aśrayā, promises all-sin removal by reading/hearing it, then introduces another old history.',[('Gaṇeśa',D),('Gods',C),('Sages',C),(K,S),(AŚ,F),('Kālānalapraśamana name in64','EPITHET'),('Vighnahara image in64',C),('Vijaya / Vighnahara site in64',L),('Vighnahara-site practitioners','FAMILY'),('Dūrvā-account readers/hearers','FAMILY')],story='gup5-story-analasura',outcome='Capture, successful cooling, named temple/site and actual withdrawal close the bounded fire-demon account.')
rel('Kālānalapraśamana name in64','source_epithet_of','Gaṇeśa',e);rel('Vighnahara image in64','installed_at','Vijaya / Vighnahara site in64',e)
power('Gods',e,v,typ='BOON',grant='Gaṇeśa',granted='Many boons; individual contents not stated in64.26.',notes='The presence of a grant is explicit; no remembered details are filled in.')
power('Vighnahara-site practitioners',e,v,mechanism='Bathing, giving, tapas and observance become inexhaustible through the deity’s favour.',trigger='These practices at the installed site.')
power('Dūrvā-account readers/hearers',e,v,mechanism='All-sin removal.',trigger='Read or hear this dūrvā account.')
